{
 "text": "The liar's strategy is to serve you something other than what you asked for. Maybe it's malware instead of the installer. Maybe it's an edited version of a document. It costs them nothing, and if you don't check, it works every time.\n\nBut we know how to check. The name is computed from the content, so when the bytes arrive, you compute their C-I-D yourself and compare. If the liar sent anything other than the real content, the C-I-D won't match, and you throw it away.\n\nThis defense is absolute in a way that's unusual in security. It doesn't depend on how clever the liar is, or how many computers they have. Any bytes other than the right ones produce a different C-I-D, unless the liar has found a genuine collision. And that's the forger's job, not the liar's. So verification takes the cheapest attack there is, and turns it into the most expensive one.",
 "text_sha256": "0985f380c0f386b293694eec6c1fa14e24daa818656cd1500ab4250308392151",
